  "body": "Found during the work on #2394 (example case turning/cone-thread).\n\n## What happens\n\nA turned profile has a fillet that is tangent to a shoulder face. The first chords of the fillet are steep in (station, radius). The Profile Rough and Profile Finish rides go far out of the bar at the station of these chords, and come back. Each rung feeds through air.\n\nReading: a 30 mm bar, a collar of 30 mm diameter to Z20, a 1.2 mm fillet from the shoulder into a cone, insert `TN-1600-0R8`, stepdown 1.0, leave 0.25. The posted rough has, for each rung:\n\n```\nG00 X354.326 Z17.2\nG01 X48.5 Z17.2 F0.0267\n...\nG01 X354.226 Z19.2 F0.05\nG01 X353.936 Z19.209 F0.05\nG01 X131.724 Z19.235 F0.05\nG01 X87.392 Z19.278 F0.05\n...\n```\n\nThe rapid radius of the operation is also 177 mm, because it is the maximum radius of the outer rung. The finish ride of the same part goes out to X47.182.\n\nThe same model without the fillet (a sharp shoulder) posts a maximum of X48.6.\n\n## Cause\n\n`crates/motion/src/turning/offset.rs`, `profile_offset`, the closure `val`: a segment of slope `b` claims `a + b * (s - s0) + d * sqrt(1 + b * b)` at each station of its own window. That is the offset of the infinite line. For a steep, short segment the tangent point of the ball is not on the segment at these stations. The correct claim there is the circle about the nearer end point.\n\nThe first chord of the fillet has a slope of 16.6. With `d` = 10.05 the claim is 167 mm above the segment.\n\nA vertical step is not affected: `segs` drops segments with equal stations.\n\n## Effect\n\n- The path is safe: the ride is too far out, never too deep.\n- The program wastes time: about 300 mm of feed in air for each rung.\n- On a lathe with a short X travel the limit gate refuses a legal program.\n- The bisection toward the spike writes many very short moves (see the related issue about zero-length blocks).\n\n## What is expected\n\nThe offset of a segment is limited to the stations where the tangent point is on the segment. Outside them the end point circles make the claim. The ride of a filleted shoulder stays within the nose radius plus the leave of the profile.\n\n## Workaround\n\nModel the shoulder as a sharp corner. The case of #2394 does this.\n\nRefs #2394\n",
